[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
**Short answer**

*Use a **`color-relief`** layer.*  
It reads the same `raster-dem` source you already have and lets you paint the DEM directly with an
`interpolate` expression over the special `["elevation"]` expression. No pre-rendered coloured tiles are needed.

Below is a complete, ready-to-copy example that shows:

1. Adding the DEM source (`type: "raster-dem"`).  
2. Adding a `color-relief` layer that tints the terrain from deep-blue (sea level) → green → brown → white.  
3. Optional tweaks (opacity, resampling, exaggeration, sky, camera pitch).

---

## 1. Add the DEM source

```js
// Assuming you already have a MapLibre map instance called `map`
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  // URL can be a TileJSON, a template string, or a PMTiles URL, etc.
  url: 'https://my.tileserver.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
  // If your DEM uses a non-default encoding (e.g. "terrarium") set it here:
  // encoding: 'terrarium',
  // maxzoom should match the source tileset
  maxzoom: 12
});
```

> **Note** – The source is *only* a `raster-dem`. Do **not** add a `raster` layer for it; the `color-relief` layer will do the drawing.

---

## 2. Add the `color-relief` layer

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hypsometric-tint',
  type: 'color-relief',          // <-- the layer type you need
  source: 'terrain-dem',
  // No `source-layer` – DEM sources have no vector layers
  paint: {
    // The colour ramp – an interpolate expression over ["elevation"]
    'color-relief-color': [
      'interpolate',
      ['linear'],
      ['elevation'],               // <-- elevation in metres (relative to the DEM datum)

      // ---- Elevation (m) → Colour ----
      // Sea level and below – deep ocean blue
      -200, '#0d1b2a',   // very deep water (optional, you can start at 0)
       0,   '#0d3b66',   // sea level – dark blue
      50,   '#1d3557',   // shallow water
     200,   '#457b9d',   // coast / shallow sea
     500,   '#a8dadc',   // low land – light cyan/greenish
    1000,   '#8ab17d',   // foothills – green
    1500,   '#6c8c5c',   // mid-elevation – darker green
    2000,   '#b08968',   // sub-alpine – brown-ish
    2500,   '#a66c4b',   // alpine – reddish-brown
    3000,   '#d9b08c',   // high-alpine – light brown / tan
    3500,   '#e5c07b',   // near-summit – pale tan
    4000,   '#ffffff'    // snow-capped peaks – white
    ],

    // Optional – make the tint semi-transparent so underlying imagery can be seen
    'color-relief-opacity': 0.85,

    // Optional – control how the DEM is sampled when the map is zoomed out
    // (available from GL-JS 5.20+)
    // 'resampling': 'linear'   // or 'nearest'
  }
});
```

### How the ramp works

* `["elevation"]` is **only valid inside** the `color-relief-color` expression.  
* The `interpolate` operator creates a smooth gradient between the stops you provide.  
* You can add as many stops as you like; the example above follows a classic hypsometric scheme:
  * **≤ 0 m** – blues (sea & shallow water)  
  * **≈ 200-1000 m** – greens (low hills, forest)  
  * **≈ 1500-3000 m** – browns (rock, alpine)  
  * **≥ 3500 m** – whites (snow)

Feel free to tweak the colours or add extra stops (e.g., a distinct “wetland” colour at 0-5 m).

---

## 3. (Optional) Show the terrain in 3-D and add a sky

If you also want a true 3-D surface you can enable terrain and pitch the camera. The colour-relief layer will drape over the mesh automatically.

```js
// Enable the DEM as a 3-D terrain mesh
map.setTerrain({
  source: 'terrain-dem',
  exaggeration: 1.0   // increase >1 to exaggerate relief, if desired
});

// Give the map some pitch so you can see the relief
map.easeTo({ pitch: 60, bearing: 0, duration: 1500 });

// Add a sky so the horizon isn’t just the page background
map.setSky({
  'sky-color': '#199EF3',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
  'fog-color': '#cce6ff',
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
});
```

> **Important:** The sky is a *root-level* object (`map.setSky()`), **not** a layer type.  
> The `color-relief` layer works both in 2-D (flat) and 3-D (terrain) modes.

---

## 4. Full minimal example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – Hypsometric Tint with color-relief</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.6.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.6.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},          // we’ll add sources programmatically
      layers: []            // same for layers
    },
    center: [0, 45],
    zoom: 5,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ DEM source
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://my.tileserver.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
      maxzoom: 12
    });

    // 2️⃣ Hypsometric colour-relief layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hypsometric-tint',
      type: 'color-relief',
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      paint: {
        'color-relief-color': [
          'interpolate',
          ['linear'],
          ['elevation'],
          -200, '#0d1b2a',
           0,   '#0d3b66',
          50,   '#1d3557',
         200,   '#457b9d',
         500,   '#a8dadc',
        1000,   '#8ab17d',
        1500,   '#6c8c5c',
        2000,   '#b08968',
        2500,   '#a66c4b',
        3000,   '#d9b08c',
        3500,   '#e5c07b',
        4000,   '#ffffff'
        ],
        'color-relief-opacity': 0.85
      }
    });

    // 3️⃣ (optional) 3-D terrain + sky
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });
    map.easeTo({ pitch: 60, duration: 1200 });
    map.setSky({
      'sky-color': '#199EF3',
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
      'fog-color': '#cce6ff',
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page in a browser, and you’ll see the DEM coloured directly on the client, with a smooth hypsometric ramp, no pre-rendered coloured tiles, and (if you kept the optional part) a 3-D view with a sky.

---

## Quick checklist

| ✅ | What you need |
|---|----------------|
| **Layer type** | `type: "color-relief"` |
| **Source** | A `raster-dem` source (the same one you already use for hillshade/terrain). |
| **Colour ramp** | `paint: { "color-relief-color": ["interpolate", ["linear"], ["elevation"], …] }` |
| **Opacity** (optional) | `"color-relief-opacity": 0.8` |
| **Resampling** (optional, GL JS ≥ 5.20) | `"resampling": "linear"` |
| **3-D** (optional) | `map.setTerrain({source:'terrain-dem'})` + pitch > 0 |
| **Sky** (optional) | `map.setSky({...})` |

That’s all you need to get a client-side hypsometric tint on a raster-DEM in MapLibre GL JS!

### Short answer  
Yes – you can generate contour lines **on-the-fly** from a `raster-dem` source.  
MapLibre GL JS itself does not contain a contour generator, but the community library **`maplibre-contour`** does exactly that: it reads the DEM tiles in a Web-Worker, builds vector contour tiles in memory, and serves them to the map through a custom protocol URL.  

Below is a step-by-step guide (with a complete working example) that shows how to:

1. **Add a `raster-dem` source** (your elevation tiles).  
2. **Create a `DemSource`** from `maplibre-contour`.  
3. **Register the source with the map** (`setupMaplibre`).  
4. **Add a vector source** that points to the contour-protocol URL.  
5. **Add a line layer** (or a label layer) that draws the contours.  

---

## 1️⃣ Prerequisites  

| What you need | Why |
|---------------|-----|
| **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 5.6** | `color-relief` and the new style spec are required for other elevation tricks, but contour generation works with any recent version. |
| **`maplibre-contour` npm package** | Provides the DEM → contour pipeline. |
| **A raster-DEM source** (e.g. Terrarium, Mapbox, or `mapzen` encoding) | The library must know the encoding to decode the elevation values correctly. |
| **A modern browser** (Web-Worker + `OffscreenCanvas` support) | The contour work is done in a background thread. |

```bash
# Using npm / yarn / pnpm
npm install maplibre-gl maplibre-contour
# or, if you prefer a CDN, see the “CDN example” section below.
```

---

## 2️⃣ Add the DEM source to the map  

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// 1️⃣ Create the map
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
  zoom: 12,
  pitch: 45,               // optional – you can keep it flat
  antialias: true
});

// 2️⃣ Add the raster-dem source (replace the URL with your own DEM tiles)
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  // The `encoding` is **not** part of the source definition – you tell the
  // contour library about it later (Terrarium, Mapbox, etc.).
  tileSize: 256,
  maxzoom: 14
});
```

> **Note** – The DEM source can be used for hillshade, 3-D terrain, **and** for contours.  
> Keep only **one** `raster-dem` source; reuse it everywhere to avoid extra network traffic.

---

## 3️⃣ Set up `maplibre-contour`  

```js
import mlcontour from 'maplibre-contour';

// 3️⃣ Create a DemSource – this is the object that does the heavy lifting.
const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
  // URL pattern must match the source you added above.
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  // Choose the correct encoding for your DEM tiles.
  //   • "terrarium"  – 16-bit elevation packed as R,G,B (Mapbox style)
  //   • "mapbox"    – 16-bit signed integer (Mapbox DEM)
  //   • "raw"       – 16-bit signed integer in a single band (e.g. GeoTIFF)
  encoding: 'terrarium',
  // Optional: limit the max zoom the worker will request.
  maxzoom: 14,
  // Run the contour generation in a Web-Worker (highly recommended).
  worker: true
});

// 4️⃣ Register the source with the map instance.
// This adds a tiny “contour://” protocol handler that the map can use.
demSource.setupMaplibre(map);
```

### What the options mean  

| Option | Description |
|--------|-------------|
| `url` | Tile URL template – **must** match the `raster-dem` source you added. |
| `encoding` | How elevation is packed in the raster. `terrarium` is the most common for Mapbox-style DEMs. |
| `maxzoom` | Highest zoom level you expect to request. The worker will never ask for tiles above this. |
| `worker` | `true` → run in a background thread (recommended). `false` → run on the main thread (simpler debugging). |

---

## 4️⃣ Add a **vector** source that points to the contour protocol  

The `DemSource` creates a custom URL scheme (`contour://`) that returns **vector tiles** containing the generated contour lines. You add a normal `vector` source that points to that URL.

```js
// 5️⃣ Add a vector source that uses the contour protocol.
map.addSource('contours', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The function builds a URL that encodes the contour parameters.
  // You can call it once and reuse the URL, or call it each time you
  // change thresholds / level keys.
  tiles: [
    demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
      // ── Contour generation parameters ────────────────────────
      // `thresholds` maps zoom → [minor, major] spacing in metres.
      thresholds: {
        // At zoom 11 we want minor every 200 m, major every 1000 m.
        11: [200, 1000],
        // At zoom 12 we want finer spacing.
        12: [100, 500],
        // At higher zooms you can go even finer.
        14: [50, 200]
      },
      // Property names that will appear on each feature.
      elevationKey: 'ele',   // numeric elevation (m)
      levelKey: 'level',     // 1 = major, 0 = minor
      // Optional: give the layer a stable name (used in `source-layer`).
      contourLayer: 'contours'
    })
  ],
  // The vector source can be queried up to this zoom.
  maxzoom: 15
});
```

### How the parameters work  

| Parameter | Effect |
|-----------|--------|
| `thresholds` | A map of **zoom → [minorSpacing, majorSpacing]** (both in metres). The library will generate two sets of lines: *minor* (thin) and *major* (thick). |
| `elevationKey` | Name of the numeric property that holds the elevation value on each feature. |
| `levelKey` | Name of the property that tells you whether the line is *major* (`1`) or *minor* (`0`). Useful for styling. |
| `contourLayer` | The name of the **source-layer** inside the generated vector tiles. You will reference this in the `line` layer’s `source-layer` property. |

You can change `thresholds` on the fly (e.g., in a UI control) by calling `demSource.contourProtocolUrl()` again with a new object and then calling `map.getSource('contours').setTiles([newUrl])`. The worker will recompute the needed tiles automatically.

---

## 5️⃣ Draw the contours with a **line** layer  

```js
// 6️⃣ Add a line layer that visualises the contours.
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-lines',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  // Must match the `contourLayer` name you gave above.
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  layout: {
    // Optional: you can make the lines appear above imagery but below labels.
    'line-cap': 'round',
    'line-join': 'round'
  },
  paint: {
    // Colour – you can use a single colour or an expression based on `level`.
    'line-color': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1, // major contour
      'rgba(0,0,0,0.8)',
      // minor contour
      'rgba(0,0,0,0.4)'
    ],
    // Width – thicker for major contours.
    'line-width': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1,
      2.0,   // major
      0.8    // minor
    ]
  }
});
```

### Optional: label the major contours  

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-labels',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1], // only major lines
  layout: {
    'symbol-placement': 'line',
    'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']],
    'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular'],
    'text-size': 12,
    'text-allow-overlap': false,
    'text-ignore-placement': false
  },
  paint: {
    'text-color': '#222',
    'text-halo-color': '#fff',
    'text-halo-width': 1
  }
});
```

Now you have **dynamic contour lines** that update automatically as the user pans/zooms, without ever having to pre-generate a vector tileset.

---

## 6️⃣ Full minimal example (HTML + JS)  

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre runtime contours</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.6.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@5.6.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <script type="module">
    import mlcontour from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/maplibre-contour@2.0.0/+esm';

    const map = new maplibregl.Map({
      container: 'map',
      style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
      center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
      zoom: 12,
      pitch: 45,
      antialias: true
    });

    map.on('load', () => {
      // 1️⃣ DEM source
      map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
        type: 'raster-dem',
        url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
        tileSize: 256,
        maxzoom: 14
      });

      // 2️⃣ Contour worker source
      const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
        url: 'https://tiles.example.com/dem/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
        encoding: 'terrarium',
        maxzoom: 14,
        worker: true
      });
      demSource.setupMaplibre(map);

      // 3️⃣ Vector source that points to the contour protocol
      map.addSource('contours', {
        type: 'vector',
        tiles: [
          demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
            thresholds: { 11: [200, 1000], 12: [100, 500], 14: [50, 200] },
            elevationKey: 'ele',
            levelKey: 'level',
            contourLayer: 'contours'
          })
        ],
        maxzoom: 15
      });

      // 4️⃣ Line layer
      map.addLayer({
        id: 'contour-lines',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'contours',
        'source-layer': 'contours',
        paint: {
          'line-color': [
            'match',
            ['get', 'level'],
            1, 'rgba(0,0,0,0.85)',
            'rgba(0,0,0,0.45)'
          ],
          'line-width': [
            'match',
            ['get', 'level'],
            1, 2,
            0.8
          ]
        }
      });

      // 5️⃣ Optional labels
      map.addLayer({
        id: 'contour-labels',
        type: 'symbol',
        source: 'contours',
        'source-layer': 'contours',
        filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1],
        layout: {
          'symbol-placement': 'line',
          'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']],
          'text-size': 12,
          'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular']
        },
        paint: {
          'text-color': '#222',
          'text-halo-color': '#fff',
          'text-halo-width': 1
        }
      });
    });
  </script>
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

Below is a **complete, copy-and-paste ready** recipe that turns the blank “page background” you see above the horizon into a real sky with a gentle haze (fog) that blends into the terrain.  
All you need is:

1. **A `raster-dem` source** (you already have it because terrain works).  
2. **`map.setTerrain()`** (already on).  
3. **`map.setSky()`** (or a `sky` object in the style JSON).  
4. **A non-zero pitch** (you already set `pitch: 70`).  

Everything else is just a few options that control colour, blending and fog.

---

## 1. Minimal working example

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – 3D terrain + sky + fog</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map (any style you like)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // ---- Base raster imagery (replace with your own) ----
        "satellite": {
          type: "raster",
          tiles: [
            "https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"
          ],
          tileSize: 256,
          maxzoom: 19
        },

        // ---- DEM source – the same one you already use ----
        "terrain-dem": {
          type: "raster-dem",
          url: "https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/tilejson.json",   // <-- your DEM TileJSON
          tileSize: 256,
          maxzoom: 12,
          // encoding must match the DEM (terrarium, mapbox, etc.)
          // encoding: "terrarium"
        }
      },

      // ---- Base layers (you can keep your own order) ----
      layers: [
        {
          id: "satellite",
          type: "raster",
          source: "satellite"
        }
        // … any other layers you want under the terrain …
      ]
    },

    center: [ -122.447303, 37.753574 ], // any lon/lat you like
    zoom: 13,
    pitch: 70,          // 👈 you already have this
    bearing: 0,
    antialias: true    // important for smooth 3-D rendering
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Enable the terrain mesh
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.setTerrain({
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      exaggeration: 1.0          // tweak if you want more “vertical” relief
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Add a sky (root-level object, NOT a layer)
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.setSky({
      // Sky colour – a light blue that fades to white at the horizon
      'sky-color': '#88c0d0',          // you can use any CSS colour or hex
      // How quickly the sky colour blends into the horizon colour
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,

      // Horizon colour – usually a very light (almost white) tone
      'horizon-color': '#f8f8ff',

      // Optional: a subtle horizon-fog blend (makes the line softer)
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.3,

      // -------------------------------------------------
      // 4️⃣  Fog (the “haze” that meets the ground)
      // -------------------------------------------------
      // Fog colour – typically a desaturated blue/gray that matches the sky
      'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',

      // How much the fog colour mixes with the terrain colour near the ground
      // 0 = no fog, 1 = fully fog-coloured. 0.5 is a nice middle ground.
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.5,

      // How quickly the fog fades out with distance (0-1). 0.5 gives a gentle
      // gradient that looks like atmospheric haze.
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣  (Optional) Add a background layer so the “page”
    //     colour never shows up if the sky fails to draw.
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'background',
      type: 'background',
      paint: { 'background-color': '#e0e8f8' }   // a very light blue fallback
    }, 'satellite'); // insert below your raster base layer
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What each part does

| Step | What it does | Why it matters for the sky |
|------|--------------|----------------------------|
| **`map.setTerrain()`** | Builds a mesh from the `raster-dem` source. | The sky only renders when a terrain mesh exists (it tells the renderer that the view is 3-D). |
| **`map.setSky()`** | Adds a *root-level* `sky` object (not a layer). | This is the only way MapLibre draws a sky; there is **no** `type: "sky"` layer. |
| **`sky-color` / `horizon-color`** | Define the colour of the dome and the line where it meets the ground. | Gives you a blue-sky look; you can make it sunset-orange, night-black, etc. |
| **`sky-horizon-blend`** | Controls how quickly the sky colour fades into the horizon colour. | A value around `0.4-0.6` looks natural. |
| **`fog-color`** | The colour of the atmospheric haze that appears close to the terrain. | Gives the “ground meets sky” effect you asked for. |
| **`fog-ground-blend`** | How strongly the fog colour mixes with the terrain colour. | 0.4-0.6 is a good starting point; increase for a thicker haze. |
| **`horizon-fog-blend`** (optional) | Softens the hard line where fog meets the horizon. | Makes the transition smoother, especially on steep terrain. |
| **`background` layer** | Guarantees a colour under the sky if the sky fails to draw (e.g., on very old browsers). | Not required, but a nice safety net. |

---

## 2. Quick checklist – why the sky might still be missing

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **No sky at all (just the page colour)** | `map.setTerrain()` never called or the DEM source failed to load. | Verify the DEM URL, check the network tab, and make sure `map.setTerrain({source:'terrain-dem'})` runs after `map.on('load')`. |
| **Sky appears but is completely black** | `sky-color`/`horizon-color` set to a dark value or omitted. | Provide explicit light colours as in the example. |
| **Fog looks like a solid block covering the whole map** | `fog-ground-blend` set to `1` (or a very high value). | Use a value between `0.3` and `0.6`. |
| **Sky only shows when you tilt the map, but disappears when you go back to `pitch: 0`** | This is expected – the sky is only drawn when the view is 3-D. Keep a non-zero pitch for the effect you want. |
| **Error `setSky is not a function`** | Using a MapLibre version older than **v2.4** (the sky API was added in v2.4). | Upgrade to the latest MapLibre GL JS (≥ 2.4, current 4.x). |
| **`sky-type` or `sky-atmosphere-sun` properties do nothing** | Those are **Mapbox-only** properties; MapLibre ignores them. | Remove them; only the properties listed in `map.setSky()` are honoured. |

---

## 3. Tweaking the look – a few common variations

### Night sky

```js
map.setSky({
  'sky-color': '#0b1d2e',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.2,
  'horizon-color': '#1a2b3c',
  'fog-color': '#0c1e2f',
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.4
});
```

### Sunset / golden-hour

```js
map.setSky({
  'sky-color': '#ffb347',          // warm orange
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.6,
  'horizon-color': '#ffcc33',
  'fog-color': '#ffdd99',
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
});
```

### Minimal fog (just a thin haze)

```js
map.setSky({
  'sky-color': '#87ceeb',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
  'horizon-color': '#f0f8ff',
  'fog-color': '#cce6ff',
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.2   // very light haze
});
```

Feel free to experiment with the numeric values; they are all linear blends between `0` (no effect) and `1` (full effect).

---

## 4. Where to read more (official docs)

| Topic | Link |
|-------|------|
| `sky` object spec (all supported properties) | <https://maplibre.org/maplibre-style-spec/sky/> |
| `setSky()` API | <https://maplibre.org/maplibre-gl-js/docs/API/classes/Map/#setsky> |
| 3-D terrain guide (including `queryTerrainElevation`) | <https://maplibre.org/maplibre-gl-js/docs/examples/3d-terrain/> |
| Full style spec (version 8) | <https://maplibre.org/maplibre-style-spec/> |

---

### TL;DR

1. **Enable terrain** (`map.setTerrain({source:'terrain-dem'})`).  
2. **Add a sky** with `map.setSky({ … })` – set `sky-color`, `horizon-color`, `sky-horizon-blend`, plus `fog-color` and `fog-ground-blend` for the haze.  
3. Keep a **non-zero pitch** (you already have `70°`).  
4. Optionally add a background layer as a safety net.

That’s it – the blank page background above the horizon will be replaced by a proper sky with atmospheric fog that blends into the terrain. Happy mapping!

Below is a step-by-step recipe for putting a **pre-rendered shaded-relief basemap** (JPEG/PNG tiles) behind all of your vector data (roads, parks, labels, etc.) in a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1.  What you’re actually adding

* A **raster source** (`type: "raster"`).  
* A **raster layer** (`type: "raster"`).  

Because the tiles are already shaded-relief images, MapLibre does **not** treat them as a DEM – they are just pictures that sit in the layer stack like any other raster background.

---

## 2.  Minimal working example

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Shaded-relief basemap + vector data</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map (any style you like)
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // a dummy empty source – we’ll add the raster source later
        "empty": { type: "geojson", data: { type: "FeatureCollection", features: [] } }
      },
      layers: [
        // keep a background so you never see a black canvas
        { id: "bg", type: "background", paint: { "background-color": "#e0e0e0" } }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Add the raster-DEM-like (shaded-relief) source
  // -------------------------------------------------
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.addSource('shaded-relief', {
      type: 'raster',
      // URL template that returns JPEG tiles.
      // Example: https://my.tileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg
      tiles: ['https://my.tileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg'],
      tileSize: 256,          // most raster tiles are 256 px
      // optional: if your tiles are in a different projection (unlikely)
      // you can set `scheme: "tms"` for TMS-style Y origin.
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Add the raster layer that draws the tiles
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'relief-basemap',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'shaded-relief',
      paint: {
        // If you want the relief a little transparent so underlying background shows:
        // "raster-opacity": 0.95,
        // If you need to brighten/darken the image:
        // "raster-brightness-min": 0.8,
        // "raster-contrast": 1.2
      }
    }, /* before */ 'empty');   // <-- insert it before the first real layer

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣  Add your vector source(s) and layers
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // Example: OpenStreetMap vector tiles from a public endpoint
    map.addSource('osm', {
      type: 'vector',
      url: 'https://basemaps.cartocdn.com/rastertiles/voyager/{z}/{x}/{y}.png' // replace with your own vector source
    });

    // Roads (line layer)
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'roads',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'osm',
      'source-layer': 'transportation',
      paint: {
        'line-color': '#ffffff',
        'line-width': 1.5
      }
    });

    // Labels (symbol layer) – this will automatically be on top
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'place-labels',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'osm',
      'source-layer': 'place',
      layout: {
        'text-field': ['get', 'name'],
        'text-size': 12,
        'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular']
      },
      paint: {
        'text-color': '#000000',
        'text-halo-color': '#ffffff',
        'text-halo-width': 1
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| **1️⃣** | Starts a MapLibre map with a *bare-bones* style (just a background). |
| **2️⃣** | Registers a **raster source** that points at your JPEG tiles. |
| **3️⃣** | Adds a **raster layer** that draws those tiles. The second argument to `addLayer` (`'empty'`) tells MapLibre to insert the relief layer **before** the first real layer, i.e. at the bottom of the stack. |
| **4️⃣** | Adds any number of vector sources/layers (roads, parks, labels, …). Because they are added **after** the raster layer, they automatically sit on top. |

---

## 3.  Layer-order tricks you may need

### a) Insert the raster layer *exactly* where you want it

If you already have a style JSON with many layers, you can place the relief layer relative to an existing layer name:

```js
// Insert just above the background but below everything else:
map.addLayer(reliefLayer, 'background');

// Insert just below a specific vector layer (e.g. "water"):
map.addLayer(reliefLayer, 'water');
```

### b) Move an existing layer after the fact

If you added the raster layer first and later realized it’s on top of something, you can reposition it:

```js
// Move the relief layer to be the first (bottom) layer:
map.moveLayer('relief-basemap', map.getStyle().layers[0].id);
```

### c) Adjust opacity / brightness

Sometimes a pre-rendered relief looks too dark when draped over a bright basemap. Use the raster paint properties:

```js
map.setPaintProperty('relief-basemap', 'raster-opacity', 0.85);
map.setPaintProperty('relief-basemap', 'raster-brightness-min', 0.7);
map.setPaintProperty('relief-basemap', 'raster-contrast', 1.2);
```

All of these are **paint** properties, so they can be animated or data-driven if you ever need that.

---

## 4.  Things to keep in mind

| Issue | Why it matters | Fix / tip |
|-------|----------------|-----------|
| **Tile format** | MapLibre expects the tiles to be plain images (JPEG/PNG/WebP). If you accidentally serve a *DEM* (e.g. Terrarium or Mapbox-terrain-rgb) as JPEG, you’ll just see a garbled picture. | Verify the URL really returns a visual image. |
| **CORS** | Browsers block cross-origin image requests unless the server sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` (or your domain). | Add the header on the tile server, or host the tiles on the same origin. |
| **Tile size** | Most raster sources use 256 px tiles. If yours are 512 px you must set `tileSize: 512`. | `map.addSource('shaded-relief', {type:'raster', tiles:[...], tileSize:512});` |
| **Projection** | MapLibre works only in Web Mercator (EPSG:3857). If your tiles are in another projection they will appear in the wrong place. | Re-project the tiles on the server side, or use a custom source type (outside the scope of this skill). |
| **Labels disappearing** | If you add the raster layer *after* your label layers, the labels will be hidden underneath. | Always add the raster layer **first**, or explicitly move it below the label layer with `moveLayer`. |
| **Performance** | Raster layers are cheap, but if you have many high-resolution JPEG tiles at high zoom you may see a brief “flash” while they load. | Enable `prefetchZoomDelta` on the map (`map.setPrefetchZoomDelta(2)`) or use a CDN with HTTP/2. |

---

## 5.  Full-style JSON version (if you prefer a static style)

If you maintain your map via a style JSON file (e.g. hosted on a server or in MapLibre Studio), the same thing looks like this:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "relief": {
      "type": "raster",
      "tiles": ["https://my.tileserver.com/relief/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg"],
      "tileSize": 256
    },
    "osm": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "mapbox://mapbox.mapbox-streets-v8"
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "bg",
      "type": "background",
      "paint": { "background-color": "#e0e0e0" }
    },
    {
      "id": "relief-basemap",
      "type": "raster",
      "source": "relief",
      "paint": {
        "raster-opacity": 0.95
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "water",
      "type": "fill",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "water",
      "paint": { "fill-color": "#a0c8f0" }
    },
    {
      "id": "roads",
      "type": "line",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "transportation",
      "paint": {
        "line-color": "#ffffff",
        "line-width": 1.5
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "place-labels",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "osm",
      "source-layer": "place",
      "layout": {
        "text-field": ["get", "name"],
        "text-size": 12,
        "text-font": ["Open Sans Regular", "Arial Unicode MS Regular"]
      },
      "paint": {
        "text-color": "#000",
        "text-halo-color": "#fff",
        "text-halo-width": 1
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

Load it with:

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://my.server.com/relief-style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

---

## 6.  TL;DR checklist

1. **Add a raster source** that points at your JPEG tiles.  
2. **Add a raster layer** using that source.  
3. **Insert the raster layer before any vector layers** (or move it below them later).  
4. (Optional) Tweak `raster-opacity`, `raster-brightness-min`, `raster-contrast` to blend nicely.  
5. Ensure **CORS** headers are present on the tile server.  
6. Verify the map’s **layer order** so labels stay on top.

That’s it—your beautiful pre-rendered shaded-relief basemap will now sit snugly beneath all of your interactive vector data, with labels and other symbols rendered on top. Happy mapping!
